.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_GET 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_get \- block until the future has a result
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void *ctpool_future_get(ctpool_future *f);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Blocks until the task that this future belongs to stops, then returns
the void* value that the task function returned. If
ctpool_shutdown_immediate cancelled the task, the return value is NULL
and ctpool_future_cancelled(f) returns true.

This call does not free the future; the caller must call
ctpool_future_free after it reads the result.

Any number of threads can be inside this call on one future at the same
time, and every one of them receives the same result. Each of them
holds a reference of its own for the length of its call, so the future
outlives every waiter that is inside it, whatever order the other
references go in. The reference of the task goes when the task stops,
the reference of the caller goes with ctpool_future_free, and whichever
thread leaves the future last is the one that destroys it.

Result ownership: the pool never allocates, copies or frees the void*
that comes back; it only carries the pointer that the task function
returned. ctpool_future_free releases the handle of the future, never
the result. Once this function returns, the caller owns the result and
must free it in the way that the task function allocated it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
f   Future handle (NULL is a no-op that returns NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
The void* value returned by the task function, or NULL if the future
was cancelled or f is NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_future_done (3),
.BR ctpool_future_cancelled (3),
.BR ctpool_future_free (3)
